== Who is Buffy ==
Buffy is the main agent and the orchestrator: it decides what to read, which file to change, which command to run, and when to hand a smaller job to a helper.

Buffy is not a character with a backstory. It is the working name of the agent, and the same name is used for the orchestrator in [https://codebuff.com/docs Codebuff], the framework Freebuff is built on.

What Buffy can reach (depending on the surface, the mode and your connections):
* The files in your project
* A terminal, to run commands — including a terminal panel you can type into yourself
* A browser, for clicking, screenshots, console and network errors
* The web, for documentation and current information
* The apps you connected, such as GitHub, Linear, Notion or Google Drive
* MCP servers you added and approved

== How it works ==
Freebuff's main loop is called '''base3''': one agent investigates, edits and verifies with its own tools. A turn looks like this:

# '''You ask''' for something, in your own words.
# '''Investigate''' — Buffy lists, searches and reads before changing anything.
# '''Decide''' — it picks one action: a tool call, a question, or an answer.
# '''Read the result''' — output goes back into context, including errors and empty results.
# '''Repeat or report''' — it continues until done, blocked, or in need of you.

The instructions tell the agent to check its own result and to report checks it could not run. That is a rule it follows, not an independent audit: when a reply says "fixed", the evidence is the diff and the commands that actually ran.

== Helper agents ==
Helper agents are additions to the main loop, not the default shape of a turn. At launch Freebuff shipped '''9 specialized subagents''' orchestrated automatically; named ones include:

* '''code-reviewer''' — reads a change back and looks for problems.
* '''browser-use''' — drives a real browser.
* '''file-picker''' — finds which files matter for the request.
* '''thinker-with-files-gemini''' — deep thinking over your files.
* a '''research''' helper — reads documentation and current pages.

Limits, from the documentation:
* Up to '''6 helpers''' in a single spawn request.
* Up to '''20 code subagents''' at once with Projects access.
* Full code subagents are not offered in Plan mode and not on sponsored runs.
* Small or tightly coupled work is usually faster done by the main agent alone.

== Modes ==
{| class="wikitable"
! Mode !! What Buffy may do
|-
| '''Plan''' || Read and explain only. File writes and changing commands are refused.
|-
| '''Build''' || Can create and change files and run commands within the task.
|}

Saying "do it" inside Plan does not quietly convert the turn into Build — you approve the plan, or ask for a revision. On Desktop the agent is also instructed not to commit, push or open a pull request unless you asked; that is a rule, not a technical lock.

''Note: the CLI is not identical. There the root agent is tied to the selected model and an internal lite mode, so check the behaviour of the version you installed.''

Shortcuts in the chat:
* <code>/interview</code> — questions first, so the request is clear before work starts.
* <code>/plan</code> — plan first, change nothing yet.
* <code>/review</code> — read the current change and look for problems.
* <code>@</code> — point at a specific file; <code>/</code> opens the command list.

The documented flow from a vague idea to a reviewable change is: <code>/interview</code> → <code>/plan</code> → implement → <code>/review</code>.

== Limits ==
* It cannot read your mind; a vague brief gives a vague diff.
* It cannot log in for you or type your passwords — better that you type secrets yourself.
* It cannot promise perfect code; check anything important.
* It does not carry context between separate chats.
* Long conversations get compressed into a summary — restate hard requirements.
* Answers depend on the model the session is using.
* Desktop is not a sandbox: commands run with your user's rights.

== Trivia ==
* "Buffy" is also the name of the '''orchestrator agent''' in [https://codebuff.com/docs Codebuff], the framework Freebuff is built on.
* The name appears in official merchandise, including the "Buffy on Fire" T-shirt.
* Freebuff uses specialized agents rather than sending every task through one model and one prompt.
* The main loop has its own name: '''base3'''.

